A: Dr. Seuss, whose real name was Theodor Geisel, was a well-known author and illustrator of children's books. His stories often contained fantastical elements and whimsical characters. These imaginative aspects can lead some to feel that he was not being truthful since his works often diverged from reality. However, his primary goal was to entertain and engage children rather than to provide factual accounts. Lying implies intent to deceive, while Dr. Seuss was predominantly focused on creativity and storytelling. Thus, it is not accurate to label him as a liar.  (Label: -1.0)

